  :adr/body "# ADR 2799008171: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8171 Pulp and Papermaking Plant Operators pulp-and-papermaking-plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8171\n-- Pulp and Papermaking Plant Operators** only, Wave3 (production/\ntrades) of the rollout: Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3 batches 1-13\nalready landed 78 occupations prior to this one.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8171\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone (with the `kotoba-lang/technology`\nsibling checkout at the relative path its `deps.edn` expects) before\nany work began, via `clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation)\n(println (kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"8171\\\"))\")`, then\nre-verified with `pr-str` and `type` to confirm exact string values\n(not just non-quoted `println` output): `:id \"8171\"`, `:name \"Pulp and\nPapermaking Plant Operators\"` (both `java.lang.String`) -- confirming\nthe premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity`\nwas `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and `:operating-states\n[:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and no `:repo`/\n`:business-id`; no repository existed at `cloud-itonami/\ncloud-itonami-isco-8171` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before scaffolding).\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `8171` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\n**Reference repo.** [`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131)\n(Chemical Products Plant and Machine Operators) was confirmed live,\n`:implemented`, and was cloned and read in full this session -- its\n`store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, full doc set, and closed-\nallowlist/scope-exclusion pattern for a comparable chemical-hazard/\nheavy-machinery life-safety-critical occupation were mirrored closely,\nadapting the exclusion language to pulp-digestion/paper-forming-plant\nterms.\n\n**This actor carries an industrial-plant chemical/heavy-machinery\nhazard framing, comparable in stakes to the already-landed chemical-\nplant (8131) actor.** Pulp and Papermaking Plant Operators run\nlarge-scale pulp digestion and paper-forming plant equipment --\nsignificant hazards from chemical pulping agents (e.g. kraft/sulfite\ncooking liquors), high-temperature/high-pressure process equipment\n(digesters), and heavy rotating machinery (paper-forming rollers,\npresses, calenders), categorically higher-stakes than ordinary\nworkshop trades. The closed op allowlist therefore must NEVER include\nany op that directly finalizes a plant-operation-execution decision or\na plant-safety-clearance decision, nor override a plant safety\nofficer's judgment -- these are always a hard, permanent, structural\nblock (absent from the allowlist entirely, not merely gated), never\nauto-commit-eligible under any confidence level.\n\n**This actor coordinates PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it never\noperates plant equipment itself and never makes a plant-operation-\nexecution or plant-safety-clearance decision.** It is not an authority\nto decide when or whether pulp digestion or paper-forming proceeds; it\nis a coordination layer for production-run/batch/progress record\nlogging, crew/shift scheduling, safety-concern surfacing and\nadministrative/equipment supply order coordination (never pulping\nchemicals themselves -- chemical handling is entirely out of scope for\nthis administrative-coordination actor), all gated behind an\nindependent governor.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8171` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for pulp and papermaking plant\noperators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Pulp &\nPapermaking Plant Scheduling & Logistics Coordination Advisor ⊣\nPulpCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference repo's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger. All source is\nportable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests are `clojure.test` `.clj`,\nmatching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/batch/progress data logging\n  (administrative metadata only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a process-anomaly/chemical-\n  exposure/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible, no confidence-level exception, ever\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- administrative/equipment procurement\n  proposal (NOT pulping chemicals themselves -- chemical handling is\n  entirely out of scope for this administrative-coordination actor),\n  escalates above a registered cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes a plant-operation-execution\ndecision, finalizes a plant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a\nplant safety officer's judgment -- these decision classes are\nstructurally absent from the allowlist, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`pulpcoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-plant** -- the operator/plant record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never operates pulp-\n   digestion or paper-forming plant equipment).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **plant-mismatch** -- if the proposal names a plant, it must be the\n   SAME plant verified for this request (defense-in-depth against a\n   proposal quietly targeting an unverified plant).\n5. **unknown-operator / operator-wrong-plant** -- if the proposal\n   references an operator, that operator must be a REGISTERED\n   certified pulp-and-papermaking-plant operator belonging to this\n   plant.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to authorize the pulping process/digester to\n   proceed, to finalize a plant-operation or plant-safety-clearance\n   decision, or to override a plant safety officer's judgment is a\n   HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content,\n   independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`pulpcoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a process-anomaly/\n  chemical-exposure/equipment-condition concern -- it never resolves\n  the concern itself, and this is unconditional: no confidence level,\n  however high, ever exempts a safety flag from human sign-off.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (20000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"pulp\", \"digester\", \"chemical\", \"paper\") can accidentally match inside\nthe mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is\nespecially sharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is pulp-and-\npapermaking-plant coordination, so bare nouns like \"pulp\"/\"digester\"/\n\"chemical\"/\"paper\"/\"batch\" are ordinary, unavoidable vocabulary.\n`pulpcoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"authorize the pulping\nprocess to proceed\", \"finalize the plant-operation decision\",\n\"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"), never bare nouns, and\n`pulpcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names an \"unresolved\nchemical-exposure concern near digester unit 3, equipment-condition\nreview pending\"), never trips the scope-exclusion guard. A companion\ntest, `scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts\ndirectly against `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a\nrationale/description containing only bare domain nouns (\"proposed\nschedule-crew-operation for batch 12 pulp-process preparation on\nplant PP-1\", \"operator fully certified for digester handling and\npaper-machine procedure documentation for this batch\") does not trip\nthe guard, while\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-pulping-process-to-proceed`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-plant-operation-decision`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-plant-safety-clearance`\nand\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-plant-safety-officer`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`,\n`holds-a-finalize-plant-operation-decision-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nand\n`holds-an-override-plant-safety-officer-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8171\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8171` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8171`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry, not\n  assumed, immediately before the edit to account for concurrent\n  sibling landings in this same batch -- six sibling agents were\n  landing occupations concurrently).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 26 tests, 66 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (mojibake scan: 0\n  replacement characters found).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-8131`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n- No op that finalizes a plant-operation-execution decision, finalizes\n  a plant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant safety\n  officer's judgment exists anywhere in this actor's code -- this is a\n  structural absence (closed allowlist), not a runtime-gated\n  exclusion, and is the load-bearing safety property this ADR\n  records.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-plant-operation-authorization`\n  or `:authorize-pulping-process` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops`: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  industrial-plant-safety guidance -- an always-escalate op still\n  means the *capability* to finalize a plant-operation-execution\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision at a plant with significant hazards from\n  chemical pulping agents, high-temperature process equipment and\n  heavy rotating machinery, the capability itself must not exist in\n  the allowlist at all -- this matches the strict bar already\n  established by `cloud-itonami-isco-8131` (chemical-process-\n  execution decisions).\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"pulp\" \"digester\"\n  \"chemical\" \"paper\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class -- this actor's entire domain is pulp-and-papermaking-plant\n  coordination, so bare nouns would self-trip on essentially every\n  legitimate proposal (work-record logs naming \"pulp batch 12 progress\n  notes\", crew-schedule proposals naming \"paper machine shift\n  changeover\", safety flags naming \"chemical-exposure concern\",\n  supply orders naming \"plant PPE and administrative digester-unit\n  procurement signage\", etc.). Scope-exclusion terms were phrased\n  exclusively as multi-word finalization/execution ACTION phrases\n  instead, per the task's explicit self-tripping-bug-pattern guidance,\n  and a dedicated regression test asserts the default mock advisor's\n  proposals across every allowlisted op never self-trip.\n- Checking plant/operator provenance only from the proposal (never the\n  request): rejected -- the plant-mismatch check requires the plant\n  identity to be anchored at the request level and independently\n  cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so a proposal\n  that quietly names a different, unverified plant is caught even if\n  the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the operator-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no operator to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the plant, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references an `:operator-id`.\n- Allowing `:coordinate-supply-order` to cover pulping chemicals:\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit scope guidance -- this\n  actor coordinates PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY, and pulping-\n  chemical handling is entirely out of scope; the op's docstring and\n  every fixture in this repo name only plant-equipment/administrative-\n  supply items (plant PPE, procurement signage), never pulping\n  chemicals themselves.\n- Using an occupation outside the 81xx plant-and-machine-operator\n  subgroup as the structural template: rejected -- `8131` (Chemical\n  Products Plant and Machine Operators) matches this occupation's\n  pre-existing `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n  :audit-ledger]` exactly and carries the closest comparable chemical/\n  heavy-machinery industrial-plant hazard framing already landed in\n  this catalog, so it was used as the template instead."}]
